{
  "dom_id": "step-t3383",
  "name": "Dividing a Polynomial Using Synthetic Division",
  "problem": "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c).$ Here's how we do it.\n\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/c1b0377fa935d61d4e74ffc1c78053dfd3dd8f263568332d620dcb93759946df.svg) | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/3f485a01bba1f4e18787a41ab04f0a8fbdd4109ebedf46a187dafeef99b385d4.svg) | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}.$ |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/69548b3acc3814a7acec5ba948ef87e9fa5dca46d812ebe2d9fec5a2e175b944.svg) | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/c269366095219f44183b05a45d47c6ca588716c308e848bc0b2a34e371f7c0f5.svg) | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}.$ |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/eb6c7df31edcb133c5b6ab6b253d76c65fc91a84d9210d9d5e21db095526def5.svg) | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}.$ |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/7aafdb77f7ad5344a8e43bf1321e236fac2ec54a8057078d132443e173ae8e4a.svg) | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}.$ |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14208915/assets/96a542425a9348da58254d2709a2e54c8bfbbe9476f470c4828918c601c53aba.svg) | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}.$ Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}},$ which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2,$ $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4.\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case. \n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3} ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
